,title,link,text,language,family
0,Maya music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maya_music,"Although no written music has survived, archaeologists have excavated musical instruments and painted and carved depictions of the ancient Maya that show how music was a complex element of societal and religious structure.",Tzotzil,Mayan
1,Tzotzil - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tzotzil,"The Tzotzil conceive the World as a square, at whose center is the ""navel"", a mound of earth located in the ceremonial center. The world rests on the shoulders of the Vashak, analogous to the Four-Corner Gods or Sky-Bearers of the ancient Maya.",Tzotzil,Mayan
2,"teleSUR Reports - Vayijel: Rock in Tzotzil - YouTubeTzotzil - Fiesta Music - YouTubeTzotzil Maya musicians in Zinacantán | National Museum of the ...Tzotzil - WikipediaModern Mayan: The Indian Music of Chiapas, Mexico, Vol. 1Maya music - WikipediaIndigenous Maya Band Sak Tzevul Rocks Colgate",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4W7mr7HCMNQ,"Jun 22, 2026 · ""Guardian Animal - The Rock of Vayijel"" rock music sung in Tzotzil using traditional instruments. Tzotzil is the language of the indigenous Maya community of... May 30, 2015 · 43 Share 2.7K views 10 years ago Provided to YouTube by Smithsonian Folkways Recordings Tzotzil - Fiesta Music · Tzotzil harpist The images depict scenes of everyday life among the Chan Santa Cruz Maya of Quintana Roo State and the Chamula Maya and Tzotzil Maya, in Chiapas State. Also present are views of archaeological sit... The Tzotzil conceive the World as a square, at whose center is the ""navel"", a mound of earth located in the ceremonial center. The world rests on the shoulders of the Vashak, analogous to the Four-Corner Gods or Sky-Bearers of the ancient Maya. Featuring some languages of Chiapas, including Tzotzil, Tzeltal, Ch’ol, and Tojolab’al, some tracks explore pantheistic Catholicism with borrowings from the Spanish language, while others are modern versions of Mayan incantations. Although no written music has survived, archaeologists have excavated musical instruments and painted and carved depictions of the ancient Maya that show how music was a complex element of societal and religious structure. Apr 18, 2024 · Their distinctive style, known as Bats’i Rock, is rooted in the rich traditions of the Tzotzil Maya people of Chiapas, Mexico, and emerged as a response to centuries of nationalistic narratives that pitted Indigenous culture against modern progress.",Tzotzil,Mayan
